<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0">
  <head><title>Chemistry: From Atoms to the Frontier</title></head>
  <body>
    <outline text="Chemistry: From Atoms to the Frontier" _note="Open source: https://github.com/BrianY28/subjectmap">
      <outline text="Foundations and atoms" _note="1700s-present · Measurement, conservation and atomic structure provide the language used throughout chemistry.">
        <outline text="Chemical measurement" _note="1700s-present&#10;Chemical claims become testable when quantities, units and uncertainty are reported together. Measurements connect macroscopic observations to models of matter.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 1.4 Measurements. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/1-4-measurements&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 1.5 Measurement uncertainty accuracy and precision. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/1-5-measurement-uncertainty-accuracy-and-precision">
          <outline text="Units and dimensions" _note="A numerical value needs a unit, and dimensional analysis checks whether an equation compares compatible quantities."/>
          <outline text="Accuracy and precision" _note="Agreement with a reference and agreement among repeated measurements describe different aspects of measurement quality."/>
          <outline text="Uncertainty" _note="An uncertainty estimate describes a range of plausible values under stated assumptions, rather than a guarantee of exactness."/>
          <outline text="Calibration" _note="Reference materials and calibration measurements connect instrument response to the quantity being measured."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Stoichiometry and the mole" _note="1700s-present&#10;Stoichiometry uses balanced chemical equations to relate amounts consumed and produced. The mole connects a count of specified entities to laboratory-scale quantities.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 4.3 Reaction stoichiometry. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-3-reaction-stoichiometry&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 4.4 Reaction Yields. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-4-reaction-yields&#10;- BIPM. SI base unit: mole. https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole">
          <outline text="Amount of substance" _note="One mole contains exactly 6.02214076 × 10²³ specified entities, such as atoms, molecules or ions."/>
          <outline text="Balanced equations" _note="Stoichiometric coefficients conserve each chemical element and electric charge in a reaction equation."/>
          <outline text="Limiting reactant" _note="The reactant exhausted first fixes the maximum product amount for the assumed reaction and complete conversion."/>
          <outline text="Yield" _note="Actual isolated product can fall below the theoretical amount because of incomplete conversion, competing reactions and losses."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Atomic theory and isotopes" _note="1800s-present&#10;Atoms are identified by their proton number; isotopes of an element differ in neutron number. Atomic theory links composition measurements to microscopic structure.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 2.1 Early ideas in atomic theory. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-1-early-ideas-in-atomic-theory&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 2.3 Atomic structure and symbolism. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism">
          <outline text="Atomic number" _note="The number of protons identifies an element; changing electron number changes its ionic charge."/>
          <outline text="Isotopes" _note="Isotopes share atomic number but differ in mass number because they contain different numbers of neutrons."/>
          <outline text="Atomic weights" _note="An element’s atomic weight reflects isotopic masses and abundances, which can vary among natural samples."/>
          <outline text="Chemical conservation" _note="Ordinary chemical reactions rearrange nuclei and electrons without changing the identities of the nuclei."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Electron configurations" _note="1900s-present&#10;Quantum mechanics describes electrons using states rather than fixed planetary orbits. Electron configurations summarize orbital occupancies and help explain chemical trends.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 6.3 Development of quantum theory. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-3-development-of-quantum-theory&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 6.4 Electronic structure of atoms electron configurations. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations">
          <outline text="Orbitals" _note="An orbital is a one-electron wavefunction used in an approximate description of a many-electron atom."/>
          <outline text="Pauli exclusion" _note="At most two electrons occupy one spatial orbital, with opposite spin projections."/>
          <outline text="Hund’s rule" _note="For degenerate orbitals in the usual ground-state filling picture, parallel singly occupied states are favored before pairing."/>
          <outline text="Configuration limits" _note="Simple filling orders are useful patterns with exceptions, and ionization can change the ordering and occupancy of orbitals."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="The periodic table" _note="1860s-present&#10;Arranging elements by atomic number reveals recurring patterns related to valence-electron structure. Trends guide predictions but do not replace evidence for a particular substance.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 6.5 Periodic variations in element properties. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-5-periodic-variations-in-element-properties&#10;- IUPAC. Periodic Table of Elements. https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/">
          <outline text="Periods and groups" _note="Rows track successive shells and columns collect elements with recurring valence patterns."/>
          <outline text="Atomic size" _note="Effective nuclear attraction and shielding help explain broad size trends across and down the table."/>
          <outline text="Ionization energy" _note="Removing an electron requires energy that depends on its binding and the electronic structure of the resulting ion."/>
          <outline text="Chemical context" _note="Oxidation states and reactivity depend on bonding partners and conditions, so periodic trends have meaningful exceptions."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="States and mixtures" _note="1700s-present&#10;Matter can be classified by physical state and composition. Pure substances, solutions and heterogeneous mixtures require different descriptions and separation methods.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 1.2 Phases and classification of matter. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/1-2-phases-and-classification-of-matter&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 3.3 Molarity. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/3-3-molarity">
          <outline text="Phases" _note="A phase is a region with uniform physical and chemical properties, separated from other phases by an interface."/>
          <outline text="Pure substances" _note="Elements and compounds have defined chemical identities, unlike mixtures whose component proportions can vary."/>
          <outline text="Solutions" _note="A solution is a homogeneous mixture; specifying concentration requires stating the amount and reference basis."/>
          <outline text="Physical separation" _note="Differences in volatility, solubility or particle size can separate mixture components without changing their chemical identities."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Bonding and molecular structure" _note="1800s-present · Electronic structure explains bonding, three-dimensional molecular shape and the interactions between molecules.">
        <outline text="Ionic and covalent bonding" _note="1800s-present&#10;Chemical bonding arises when interactions between nuclei and electrons stabilize an arrangement. Ionic and covalent descriptions are useful limiting models of a wider range of electron distributions.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 7.1 Ionic bonding. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-1-ionic-bonding&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 7.2 Covalent bonding. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-2-covalent-bonding">
          <outline text="Covalent bonding" _note="Shared electron density can stabilize a pair of nuclei at a finite equilibrium separation."/>
          <outline text="Ionic solids" _note="Electrostatic interactions extend throughout an ionic lattice, so its stability is not determined by one isolated ion pair."/>
          <outline text="Bond polarity" _note="Unequal electron sharing creates partial charges; these need not equal formal charges or oxidation states."/>
          <outline text="Bond energy" _note="Breaking a bond requires energy, while forming the corresponding bond releases energy under the same reference conditions."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Lewis structures and resonance" _note="1910s-present&#10;Lewis structures track valence electrons and provide a first model of bonding. Resonance describes a single electronic structure using multiple contributing drawings.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 7.3 Lewis symbols and structures. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 7.4 Formal charges and resonance. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance">
          <outline text="Electron accounting" _note="Valence electrons are assigned to bonds and lone pairs while preserving the species’ total electron count."/>
          <outline text="Formal charge" _note="Formal charge assigns bonding electrons equally and is a bookkeeping device rather than a direct measurement of charge density."/>
          <outline text="Resonance contributors" _note="Different electron-placement drawings with the same nuclear connectivity describe delocalization, not molecules rapidly switching structures."/>
          <outline text="Octet limits" _note="Electron-deficient species, radicals and some heavier-element compounds require descriptions beyond a simple octet rule."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Molecular geometry" _note="1900s-present&#10;The spatial arrangement of nuclei influences polarity and reactivity. VSEPR gives a useful qualitative starting point for many main-group molecules.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 7.6 Molecular structure and polarity. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity">
          <outline text="Electron domains" _note="VSEPR counts bonding regions and lone pairs around a central atom to estimate an electron-domain arrangement."/>
          <outline text="Molecular shape" _note="Names such as bent or trigonal pyramidal describe nuclear positions rather than lone-pair positions."/>
          <outline text="Molecular dipole" _note="Bond-dipole vectors may reinforce or cancel according to the whole molecule’s geometry."/>
          <outline text="Model boundaries" _note="Transition-metal compounds and strongly delocalized systems often need more detailed electronic-structure models."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Molecular orbital theory" _note="1920s-present&#10;Molecular orbitals extend across a molecule and are filled with electrons subject to quantum rules. This model explains features such as delocalization and the magnetism of oxygen.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 8.4 Molecular orbital theory. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory">
          <outline text="Orbital combinations" _note="Atomic-orbital combinations can form bonding, antibonding and nonbonding molecular orbitals."/>
          <outline text="Bond order" _note="In a simple diatomic model, half the difference between bonding and antibonding occupancies estimates bond order."/>
          <outline text="Magnetism" _note="Unpaired electrons in molecular orbitals give a paramagnetic response, as in ground-state O₂."/>
          <outline text="Approximation" _note="Orbital pictures are model-dependent representations; quantitative predictions also require electron correlation and appropriate computational methods."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Intermolecular forces" _note="1800s-present&#10;Interactions between molecules help determine boiling, solubility and organization. Their combined effects depend on molecular shape, polarizability and the surrounding medium.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 10.1 Intermolecular forces. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-1-intermolecular-forces&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 11.1 The dissolution process. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-1-the-dissolution-process">
          <outline text="Dispersion" _note="Correlated fluctuations in electron distributions produce attractive dispersion interactions in all atoms and molecules."/>
          <outline text="Dipolar interactions" _note="Permanent dipoles interact in ways that depend on orientation and separation."/>
          <outline text="Hydrogen bonding" _note="A hydrogen attached to a suitable donor can interact directionally with an acceptor, influencing structure and association."/>
          <outline text="Collective properties" _note="Many competing interactions and entropy determine bulk behavior, so one bond type alone rarely predicts solubility."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Stereochemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Molecules with identical connectivity can differ in their spatial arrangements. Such differences matter when molecules encounter chiral biological or chemical environments.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 5.1 Enantiomers and the tetrahedral carbon. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/5-1-enantiomers-and-the-tetrahedral-carbon&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 5.5 Sequence rules for specifying configuration. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/5-5-sequence-rules-for-specifying-configuration">
          <outline text="Chirality" _note="A chiral object is not superimposable on its mirror image; chirality need not arise from a single tetrahedral stereocenter."/>
          <outline text="Enantiomers and diastereomers" _note="Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images, while diastereomers are stereoisomers that are not mirror images."/>
          <outline text="Configuration and conformation" _note="Conformers interconvert through motions such as single-bond rotation, while configuration distinguishes stereoisomers beyond those motions."/>
          <outline text="Stereochemical labels" _note="R/S and E/Z encode structural relationships and do not by themselves specify the sign of optical rotation."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Thermodynamics and equilibrium" _note="1800s-present · Energy, entropy and chemical potentials explain equilibrium constraints; they do not determine how quickly equilibrium is reached.">
        <outline text="Energy and enthalpy" _note="1800s-present&#10;Energy conservation connects chemical changes to heat and work. Enthalpy is useful for comparing processes carried out at constant pressure.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 5.1 Energy basics. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-1-energy-basics&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 5.3 Enthalpy. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-3-enthalpy">
          <outline text="First law" _note="With heat entering the system and work done on it defined positive, the internal-energy change is ΔU = q + w."/>
          <outline text="State functions" _note="Internal energy and enthalpy depend on state, whereas heat and work depend on the process path."/>
          <outline text="Enthalpy" _note="H = U + pV, and at constant pressure with only pressure-volume work, the heat transferred equals ΔH."/>
          <outline text="Hess’s law" _note="Reaction enthalpies can be added along a hypothetical sequence because the total enthalpy change is path-independent."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Entropy and free energy" _note="1800s-present&#10;Entropy accounts for the distribution of energy and accessible microscopic states. Gibbs energy combines energetic and entropic contributions under constant-temperature, constant-pressure conditions.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 16.2 Entropy. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-2-entropy&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 16.4 Free energy. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-4-free-energy">
          <outline text="Entropy" _note="For an isolated system, spontaneous change increases total entropy; the entropy of one subsystem can decrease."/>
          <outline text="Gibbs energy" _note="G = H − TS supplies the equilibrium criterion for a closed system at fixed temperature and pressure with no imposed non-expansion work."/>
          <outline text="Reaction Gibbs energy" _note="ΔᵣG = ΔᵣG° + RT ln Q relates the local driving force to the dimensionless activity quotient."/>
          <outline text="Thermodynamics versus kinetics" _note="A negative reaction Gibbs energy favors a direction of change but does not guarantee an observable rate."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Chemical equilibrium" _note="1800s-present&#10;At equilibrium, opposing reaction rates balance and macroscopic composition remains constant under fixed conditions. Equilibrium composition need not contain equal amounts of reactants and products.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 13.1 Chemical equilibria. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-1-chemical-equilibria&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 13.2 Equilibrium constants. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-2-equilibrium-constants">
          <outline text="Dynamic balance" _note="Microscopic forward and reverse reactions continue even when there is no net macroscopic reaction."/>
          <outline text="Mass action" _note="For a specified reaction and standard states, the dimensionless activity quotient Q equals K at equilibrium.&#10;Explainer: https://briany28.github.io/subjectmap/chemistry/explainers/equilibrium/"/>
          <outline text="Reaction direction" _note="At a given temperature, Q below K favors the forward direction and Q above K favors the reverse direction under the usual closed-system constraints."/>
          <outline text="Changing conditions" _note="Temperature changes K, while adding material at fixed temperature changes Q and can shift the equilibrium composition."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Acids and bases" _note="1800s-present&#10;Acid-base models describe proton transfer or electron-pair donation. Their predictions depend on the solvent, chemical species and equilibrium conditions.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 14.1 Bronsted lowry acids and bases. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-1-bronsted-lowry-acids-and-bases&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 14.6 Buffers. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 15.2 Lewis acids and bases. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/15-2-lewis-acids-and-bases">
          <outline text="Brønsted-Lowry model" _note="An acid donates a proton and a base accepts one, creating conjugate acid-base pairs."/>
          <outline text="Lewis model" _note="A Lewis acid accepts an electron pair and a Lewis base donates one to form an interaction or bond."/>
          <outline text="Acidity and pH" _note="pH is defined using hydrogen-ion activity; replacing activity with concentration is an approximation for sufficiently dilute solutions."/>
          <outline text="Buffers" _note="A weak acid and its conjugate base can resist limited additions of acid or base, with effectiveness constrained by composition and capacity."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Solubility and complex equilibria" _note="1800s-present&#10;Dissolution, precipitation and complex formation are coupled equilibria. Predicting dissolved amounts requires accounting for all important species and conservation constraints.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 15.1 Precipitation and dissolution. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/15-1-precipitation-and-dissolution&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 15.2 Lewis acids and bases. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/15-2-lewis-acids-and-bases">
          <outline text="Solubility product" _note="Ksp relates ion activities at equilibrium with a specified solid phase, whose activity is normally taken as one when pure."/>
          <outline text="Common-ion effect" _note="Adding a shared ion often suppresses dissolution, but competing complexation or other equilibria can change the outcome."/>
          <outline text="Complex formation" _note="Ligands can reduce a free metal-ion activity by forming dissolved complexes, altering solubility and speciation."/>
          <outline text="Mass and charge balance" _note="Equilibrium relations must be solved consistently with component balances and electroneutrality."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Phase equilibria" _note="1800s-present&#10;Different phases coexist when their components have matching chemical potentials under the imposed conditions. Phase diagrams organize the stable states and boundaries of a system.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 10.4 Phase diagrams. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-4-phase-diagrams&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 11.4 Colligative properties. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties">
          <outline text="Coexistence curves" _note="Lines in a one-component pressure-temperature diagram mark conditions where two phases coexist."/>
          <outline text="Triple and critical points" _note="A triple point permits three-phase coexistence, while a liquid-gas critical point ends their coexistence boundary."/>
          <outline text="Vapor-liquid balance" _note="At equilibrium, evaporation and condensation balance; vapor pressure depends on temperature and composition."/>
          <outline text="Mixture effects" _note="Mixing changes chemical potentials, producing effects such as freezing-point depression under the applicable dilute-solution assumptions."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Kinetics and catalysis" _note="1800s-present · Rates and reaction pathways explain chemical change in time, from elementary steps to driven reaction networks.">
        <outline text="Reaction rates" _note="1800s-present&#10;Kinetics describes how rapidly composition changes. A rate law summarizes observed dependence on concentrations and conditions within its range of validity.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 12.1 Chemical reaction rates. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-1-chemical-reaction-rates&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 12.3 Rate laws. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-3-rate-laws&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 12.4 Integrated rate laws. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-4-integrated-rate-laws">
          <outline text="Rate definition" _note="Changes in species concentration are divided by stoichiometric coefficients to define a consistent reaction rate at fixed volume."/>
          <outline text="Reaction order" _note="Concentration exponents in an empirical rate law are measured and need not equal coefficients in the overall equation."/>
          <outline text="Integrated laws" _note="A kinetic model can be integrated to predict concentration against time, with different forms for different reaction orders."/>
          <outline text="Experimental control" _note="Temperature, mixing, transport and measurement response must be considered when interpreting observed rates."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Mechanisms and rate laws" _note="1800s-present&#10;A mechanism proposes elementary steps whose sum gives the observed reaction. Agreement with one rate law supports a mechanism but rarely identifies it uniquely.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 12.6 Reaction mechanisms. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-6-reaction-mechanisms">
          <outline text="Elementary steps" _note="Molecularity counts reacting entities in a proposed elementary event rather than in the overall balanced equation."/>
          <outline text="Intermediates" _note="An intermediate is formed and consumed along the pathway and therefore cancels from the overall reaction equation."/>
          <outline text="Kinetic approximations" _note="Pre-equilibrium or steady-state approximations simplify rate equations only when their timescale assumptions hold."/>
          <outline text="Testing a mechanism" _note="Rate measurements, isotope effects, spectroscopy and product distributions provide complementary constraints on proposed steps."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Activation and transition states" _note="1900s-present&#10;Reaction rates depend on access to high-energy configurations along a pathway. Barrier models explain why a favorable overall reaction can remain slow.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 12.5 Collision theory. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-5-collision-theory&#10;- Eyring, H. (1935). The Activated Complex in Chemical Reactions. Journal of Chemical Physics, 3, 107–115. https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1749604">
          <outline text="Arrhenius behavior" _note="The approximate relation k = A exp(−Ea/RT) connects a rate constant to temperature over a suitable range."/>
          <outline text="Transition state" _note="A transition state lies near a dividing region between reactants and products and is not an isolable intermediate."/>
          <outline text="Activation free energy" _note="Transition-state theory relates rate to a free-energy barrier, including entropic as well as energetic contributions."/>
          <outline text="Model limits" _note="Tunneling, recrossing, diffusion and changes of mechanism can cause departures from simple barrier-based rate formulas."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Catalysis" _note="1800s-present&#10;A catalyst participates in a reaction pathway and is regenerated in its overall cycle. It can change reaction speed and selectivity without changing the equilibrium constant for the same overall reaction.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 12.7 Catalysis. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis">
          <outline text="Alternative pathway" _note="Catalytic steps offer a different route with favorable kinetic barriers compared with an uncatalyzed pathway."/>
          <outline text="Equilibrium unchanged" _note="Under fixed conditions a catalyst accelerates approach to equilibrium but does not alter the reaction’s thermodynamic equilibrium constant."/>
          <outline text="Homogeneous and heterogeneous" _note="A catalyst may share the reactants’ phase or act at an interface between phases."/>
          <outline text="Turnover and selectivity" _note="Useful catalytic performance depends on rate, preferred products and lifetime, including deactivation and competing pathways."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Photochemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Absorbed light prepares electronically excited states that can react differently from ground-state molecules. Chemical outcomes compete with relaxation and emission.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Braslavsky, S. E. (2007). Glossary of terms used in photochemistry, 3rd edition (IUPAC Recommendations 2006). Pure and Applied Chemistry, 79, 293–465. https://doi.org/10.1351/pac200779030293">
          <outline text="Photoexcitation" _note="A photon can raise a molecule into an accessible excited state when its energy and transition properties permit absorption."/>
          <outline text="Competing pathways" _note="Fluorescence, nonradiative relaxation, intersystem crossing and chemical reaction compete after excitation."/>
          <outline text="Quantum yield" _note="The quantum yield counts specified events per absorbed photon; it must identify the event and illumination conditions."/>
          <outline text="Light-driven chemistry" _note="Photochemical reactions exchange energy with a light field, so their operating states need not equal dark thermal equilibrium."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Reaction networks" _note="1900s-present&#10;Linked reactions couple species through shared intermediates and conservation laws. Nonlinear feedback and external driving can produce behavior absent from a single isolated reaction.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Gillespie, D. T. (1977). Exact stochastic simulation of coupled chemical reactions. Journal of Physical Chemistry, 81, 2340–2361. https://doi.org/10.1021/j100540a008&#10;- Prigogine, I. (1977). Time, Structure and Fluctuations. Nobel Lecture. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/prigogine-lecture.pdf">
          <outline text="Stoichiometric network" _note="A stoichiometric matrix records how each reaction changes each species and identifies conserved combinations."/>
          <outline text="Coupled kinetics" _note="Rate equations combine individual reaction rates to determine the time evolution of all species together."/>
          <outline text="Driven states" _note="Open systems supplied with material or energy can sustain fluxes, multiple steady states or oscillations far from equilibrium."/>
          <outline text="Molecular fluctuations" _note="For well-mixed systems with small populations, stochastic reaction models can capture fluctuations missed by deterministic concentration equations."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Inorganic chemistry" _note="1800s-present · Chemistry across the periodic table connects main-group compounds, metal complexes, extended solids and biological metal sites.">
        <outline text="Main-group chemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Main-group chemistry explores the compounds of the s- and p-block elements. Valence patterns organize their behavior while size, polarity and structure create substantial variation.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 18.1 Periodicity. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/18-1-periodicity&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 18.4 Structure and general properties of the nonmetals. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/18-4-structure-and-general-properties-of-the-nonmetals">
          <outline text="Valence patterns" _note="Recurring valence-electron configurations help explain common bonding and oxidation-state patterns."/>
          <outline text="Oxides and acid-base behavior" _note="Many nonmetal oxides are acidic and many metal oxides basic, with amphoteric and other exceptions."/>
          <outline text="Allotropy" _note="An element can form different structures with distinct properties, as illustrated by graphite and diamond."/>
          <outline text="Stability and reactivity" _note="A compound’s thermodynamic stability and the kinetic barriers to reaction must be considered separately."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Coordination compounds" _note="1800s-present&#10;Metal centers bind ligands into complexes whose properties depend on composition and geometry. Coordination chemistry connects inorganic structure with catalysis, materials and biology.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 19.2 Coordination chemistry of transition metals. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/19-2-coordination-chemistry-of-transition-metals">
          <outline text="Coordination sphere" _note="Ligand donor atoms bound to a central atom define its local coordination environment."/>
          <outline text="Denticity and chelation" _note="A ligand with multiple donor sites can bind through more than one site to form chelate rings."/>
          <outline text="Isomerism" _note="Complexes with the same formula can differ in connectivity or three-dimensional arrangement."/>
          <outline text="Stability versus lability" _note="Thermodynamic binding strength and the rate of ligand exchange are distinct properties."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Ligand fields" _note="1900s-present&#10;Ligands alter the energies and mixing of metal orbitals. Ligand-field models connect electronic structure to spectra, spin states and reactivity.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 19.3 Spectroscopic and magnetic properties of coordination compounds. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/19-3-spectroscopic-and-magnetic-properties-of-coordination-compounds">
          <outline text="Orbital splitting" _note="Different coordination geometries produce different energy patterns among the metal d orbitals."/>
          <outline text="Spin states" _note="The balance between orbital splitting and electron-pairing costs helps determine high-spin or low-spin arrangements where both are possible."/>
          <outline text="Spectroscopic transitions" _note="Electronic transitions can produce absorption bands, although charge-transfer processes also contribute strongly in many complexes."/>
          <outline text="Beyond crystal fields" _note="Ligand-field molecular-orbital descriptions include covalent interactions that a purely electrostatic crystal-field model omits."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Organometallic chemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Organometallic compounds contain bonding between metals and carbon-containing groups. Their flexible bonding and reaction pathways support many catalytic transformations.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Crabtree, R. H. (2019). The Organometallic Chemistry of the Transition Metals (7th ed.). Wiley. https://www.wiley.com/en-us/the-organometallic-chemistry-of-the-transition-metals-7th-edition-p-9781119465867&#10;- Fischer, E. O. (1973). On the Road to Carbene and Carbyne Complexes. Nobel Lecture. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/fischer-lecture.pdf">
          <outline text="Metal-carbon bonding" _note="Carbon groups can attach through localized bonds or through delocalized π systems, as in sandwich complexes."/>
          <outline text="Electron counting" _note="Valence-electron counts help organize structures and reactivity, but the 18-electron rule is a useful pattern rather than a universal law."/>
          <outline text="Elementary transformations" _note="Oxidative addition, reductive elimination and migratory insertion provide recurring steps in organometallic mechanisms."/>
          <outline text="Catalytic cycles" _note="A sequence of elementary steps converts substrates into products while regenerating an active metal species."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Solid-state chemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Extended bonding and repeated structural motifs give solids properties that cannot be inferred from isolated molecules alone. Composition, defects and preparation history all matter.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 10.5 The solid state of matter. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-5-the-solid-state-of-matter&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e. OpenStax, 10.6 Lattice structures in crystalline solids. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-6-lattice-structures-in-crystalline-solids">
          <outline text="Structural classes" _note="Ionic, molecular, covalent-network and metallic descriptions distinguish common types of solid bonding."/>
          <outline text="Lattices and unit cells" _note="A crystal structure specifies a repeating arrangement of atoms together with the lattice translations."/>
          <outline text="Defects" _note="Vacancies, substitutions and other departures from an ideal lattice can control transport and chemical behavior."/>
          <outline text="Structure-property links" _note="Bonding and electronic structure connect atomic arrangements to properties such as conductivity and mechanical response."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Bioinorganic chemistry" _note="1900s-present&#10;Biological systems use metal ions for structural, catalytic and electron-transfer functions. The surrounding ligands and protein environment tune a metal site’s behavior.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Lippard, S. J., and Berg, J. M. (1994). Principles of Bioinorganic Chemistry. University Science Books. https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780935702736/principles-of-bioinorganic-chemistry/&#10;- Goodsell, D. S. (2003). Hemoglobin. RCSB PDB Molecule of the Month. https://pdb101.rcsb.org/motm/41&#10;- Goodsell, D. S. (2004). Carbonic Anhydrase. RCSB PDB Molecule of the Month. https://pdb101.rcsb.org/motm/49">
          <outline text="Metalloproteins" _note="A protein can position a metal ion and ligands to support a specific chemical function."/>
          <outline text="Small-molecule binding" _note="Heme iron binds oxygen in transport proteins, with protein structure controlling affinity and cooperativity."/>
          <outline text="Catalytic metal sites" _note="A bound metal can activate a substrate or water molecule, as zinc does in carbonic anhydrase."/>
          <outline text="Metal homeostasis" _note="Uptake, storage and delivery regulate metal availability while limiting inappropriate binding and chemical damage."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Organic chemistry" _note="1800s-present · Functional groups, mechanisms and stereochemistry organize carbon chemistry and the planning of molecular synthesis.">
        <outline text="Functional groups" _note="1800s-present&#10;Recognizable bonding patterns organize the properties and reactions of organic molecules. Their behavior is modified by the rest of the molecule and its environment.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 3.1 Functional groups. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/3-1-functional-groups&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 6.3 Polar reactions. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/6-3-polar-reactions">
          <outline text="Structural families" _note="Alcohols, amines, carbonyl compounds and other families are recognized by characteristic atoms and bonding patterns."/>
          <outline text="Electronic effects" _note="Inductive effects and resonance influence charge distribution, acidity and the availability of electron pairs."/>
          <outline text="Nucleophiles and electrophiles" _note="Electron-rich and electron-poor reaction sites provide a useful framework for many polar mechanisms."/>
          <outline text="Context matters" _note="Steric crowding, neighboring groups and solvent can change a functional group’s reactivity or selectivity."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Substitution and elimination" _note="1900s-present&#10;Substitution replaces a group, while elimination creates a multiple bond by removing groups. Competing mechanisms depend on substrate, reagent and reaction environment.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 11 summary. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/11-summary">
          <outline text="SN2 substitution" _note="A concerted displacement involves nucleophilic attack and departure of the leaving group, with inversion at the attacked tetrahedral carbon."/>
          <outline text="SN1 substitution" _note="Ionization can form a carbocation intermediate whose stability and subsequent reactions influence rate and products."/>
          <outline text="E2 elimination" _note="A concerted elimination couples proton removal to leaving-group departure and has important geometric requirements."/>
          <outline text="Competition" _note="Basicity, nucleophilicity, solvent and steric effects influence competition among substitution and elimination pathways."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Addition reactions" _note="1800s-present&#10;Addition transforms a multiple bond by attaching groups across it. Mechanism determines which positions and faces react.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 8 summary. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/8-summary">
          <outline text="Electrophilic addition" _note="An electron-rich π bond can react with an electrophile, followed by additional bonding steps."/>
          <outline text="Regioselectivity" _note="Unsymmetrical substrates can give different constitutional products, with preference determined by the particular pathway."/>
          <outline text="Stereoselectivity" _note="Syn and anti addition describe whether new groups are delivered to the same or opposite faces of a reacting π system."/>
          <outline text="Catalytic hydrogenation" _note="A catalyst can mediate net hydrogen addition across an unsaturated bond, with selectivity influenced by catalyst and substrate."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Aromaticity and substitution" _note="1800s-present&#10;Aromaticity describes characteristic stabilization and electronic behavior in certain cyclic conjugated systems. Their reactions often preserve or restore the conjugated ring.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 15.4 Aromatic ions. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/15-4-aromatic-ions&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 16.1 Electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions bromination. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/16-1-electrophilic-aromatic-substitution-reactions-bromination&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 16.4 Substituent effects in electrophilic substitutions. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/16-4-substituent-effects-in-electrophilic-substitutions">
          <outline text="Hückel pattern" _note="For simple planar cyclic conjugated systems, 4n + 2 π electrons is a useful aromaticity criterion within the model’s limits."/>
          <outline text="Delocalization" _note="Aromatic bonding is distributed around the ring rather than alternating between localized drawings over time."/>
          <outline text="Electrophilic substitution" _note="Many aromatic rings react by temporary loss of aromaticity followed by elimination that restores it."/>
          <outline text="Substituent effects" _note="Attached groups change reaction rates and positional preferences through electronic and steric effects."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Carbonyl chemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;The polarized carbon-oxygen double bond provides both an electrophilic carbon and an oxygen with electron pairs. Neighboring groups determine which carbonyl pathways are accessible.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 18 preview of carbonyl chemistry. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/18-preview-of-carbonyl-chemistry&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 23.1 Carbonyl condensations the aldol reaction. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/23-1-carbonyl-condensations-the-aldol-reaction">
          <outline text="Nucleophilic addition" _note="A nucleophile can attack the carbonyl carbon to produce a tetrahedral arrangement before subsequent steps."/>
          <outline text="Acyl substitution" _note="Carbonyl compounds with suitable leaving groups can undergo addition followed by elimination that restores a carbonyl bond."/>
          <outline text="Enols and enolates" _note="An α hydrogen next to a carbonyl can enable enol or enolate formation and reactions at the adjacent carbon."/>
          <outline text="Carbon-carbon bond formation" _note="An enol or enolate reacting with another carbonyl compound provides the foundation of aldol chemistry."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Organic synthesis" _note="1800s-present&#10;Synthesis plans a sequence of transformations that builds a target molecule with the required structure and purity. Successful routes balance selectivity with practical constraints.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Corey, E. J. (1990). The Logic of Chemical Synthesis: Multistep Synthesis of Complex Carbogenic Molecules. Nobel Lecture. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/corey-lecture.pdf&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry (10th ed.). OpenStax, 9.9 An introduction to organic synthesis. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/9-9-an-introduction-to-organic-synthesis">
          <outline text="Retrosynthesis" _note="Working backward from a target identifies simpler precursors and candidate bond-forming transformations."/>
          <outline text="Chemoselectivity" _note="A reaction must distinguish the intended functional group from other reactive groups in a molecule."/>
          <outline text="Stereocontrol" _note="A route must account for the creation, preservation or separation of the required stereoisomers."/>
          <outline text="Route evaluation" _note="Yield, step count, separations, waste and reproducibility all affect whether a proposed sequence is useful."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Analytical chemistry" _note="1700s-present · Analytical chemistry connects observations to chemical identity and amount, using representative samples, calibrated instruments and explicit uncertainty.">
        <outline text="Measurement and uncertainty" _note="1800s-present&#10;A measured value is meaningful only with its method, units and uncertainty. Sampling and calibration can dominate the error even when an instrument produces many decimal places.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Taylor, B. N., and Kuyatt, C. E. (1994). Guidelines for Evaluating and Expressing the Uncertainty of NIST Measurement Results. NIST Technical Note 1297, 1994 edition. https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/2017/05/09/tn1297s.pdf&#10;- Harvey, D. (2016). Analytical Chemistry 2.1, Chapters 4-7. DePauw University. https://sites.google.com/depauw.edu/dth/analytical-curriculum-projects/analytical-chemistry-2-1">
          <outline text="Accuracy and precision" _note="Small scatter does not rule out a shared bias, so replicate measurements alone cannot establish accuracy."/>
          <outline text="Calibration" _note="A relation between signal and reference values lets an instrument estimate an unknown within a validated range."/>
          <outline text="Uncertainty propagation" _note="Combine relevant uncertainty contributions with their sensitivities and correlations rather than merely adding significant figures."/>
          <outline text="Representative sampling" _note="Sampling, blanks and reference materials test whether the result describes the intended material rather than contamination or a selected portion."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Titration and gravimetry" _note="1700s-1900s&#10;Classical quantitative methods relate a known reaction stoichiometry to a measured volume or mass. Reliable results depend on reaction completeness, selectivity and a well-defined measurement endpoint.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 4.5 Quantitative Chemical Analysis. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-5-quantitative-chemical-analysis&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 14.7 Acid-Base Titrations. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-7-acid-base-titrations">
          <outline text="Equivalence and endpoint" _note="The stoichiometric equivalence point is a chemical condition, while an indicator or instrument reports an endpoint that may differ from it."/>
          <outline text="Titration curves" _note="Changes in pH or another measured property reveal reaction progress and help choose a suitable endpoint method."/>
          <outline text="Gravimetric analysis" _note="The measured mass of a compound with known composition can determine the amount of an analyte."/>
          <outline text="Interference and purity" _note="Co-precipitation, incomplete reaction and competing species can bias the result even when weighing and volume measurements are precise."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Chromatography" _note="1900s-present&#10;Chromatography separates mixture components through different interactions with a moving phase and a stationary phase. Retention and detector response provide complementary information about identity and amount.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Harvey, D. (2016). Analytical Chemistry 2.1, Chapter 12: Chromatographic and Electrophoretic Methods. DePauw University. https://sites.google.com/depauw.edu/dth/analytical-curriculum-projects/analytical-chemistry-2-1">
          <outline text="Partition and adsorption" _note="Different affinities for the two phases produce different migration speeds."/>
          <outline text="Resolution" _note="Separation depends on peak spacing and peak width, so a longer retention time alone does not guarantee a better separation."/>
          <outline text="Gas and liquid chromatography" _note="The choice of mobile phase and separation conditions depends on volatility, stability and analyte interactions."/>
          <outline text="Identification and quantification" _note="Retention time needs supporting evidence for identity, while concentration estimates require calibration and control of detector response."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Molecular spectroscopy" _note="1800s-present&#10;Molecules absorb, emit or scatter radiation through transitions between energy levels. Spectral position and intensity connect molecular structure and environment to measurable signals.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry, 12.5 Spectroscopy and the Electromagnetic Spectrum. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/12-5-spectroscopy-and-the-electromagnetic-spectrum&#10;- Harvey, D. (2016). Analytical Chemistry 2.1, Chapter 10: Spectroscopic Methods. DePauw University. https://sites.google.com/depauw.edu/dth/analytical-curriculum-projects/analytical-chemistry-2-1">
          <outline text="Vibrational spectra" _note="Infrared absorption and Raman scattering probe vibrations through different selection rules and can provide complementary structural evidence."/>
          <outline text="Electronic transitions" _note="UV-visible spectra probe electronic excitations whose energies depend on bonding, conjugation and environment."/>
          <outline text="Beer-Lambert relation" _note="A = εlc relates absorbance to concentration for suitable dilute, homogeneous samples at a specified wavelength, with deviations outside its assumptions."/>
          <outline text="Spectral interpretation" _note="Bands can overlap and shift with conditions, so assigning a structure usually requires multiple observations."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Nuclear magnetic resonance" _note="1940s-present&#10;NMR observes transitions of spin-active nuclei in a magnetic field. Chemical shifts, couplings and correlations help determine molecular environments, connectivity and dynamics.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry, 13.1 Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/13-1-nuclear-magnetic-resonance-spectroscopy&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry, Chapter 13 Summary. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/13-summary">
          <outline text="Chemical shift" _note="Electronic shielding changes a nucleus's resonance position relative to a reference, conventionally reported in parts per million."/>
          <outline text="Spin-spin coupling" _note="Interactions between nuclear spins can split signals and reveal connectivity, although simple splitting rules have limited domains."/>
          <outline text="Signal integration" _note="Under suitable acquisition and relaxation conditions, integrated proton signals estimate relative numbers of contributing nuclei."/>
          <outline text="Multidimensional NMR" _note="Correlations between nuclei help disentangle overlapping signals and establish structural relationships."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Mass spectrometry" _note="1900s-present&#10;Mass spectrometry measures ions by their mass-to-charge ratio, m/z. Ionization, isotope patterns and fragmentation provide evidence about composition and structure rather than an automatic unique identification.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry, 12.2 Interpreting Mass Spectra. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/12-2-interpreting-mass-spectra&#10;- Murray, K. K., et al. (2013). Definitions of terms relating to mass spectrometry (IUPAC Recommendations 2013). Pure and Applied Chemistry, 85, 1515-1609. https://doi.org/10.1351/PAC-REC-06-04-06">
          <outline text="Ionization" _note="Methods differ in the ions and fragments they produce, and a neutral molecule is not detected directly."/>
          <outline text="Mass-to-charge ratio" _note="An observed peak depends on both ion mass and charge, including adduct formation and multiple charging."/>
          <outline text="Isotopes and accurate mass" _note="Resolved masses and isotope patterns constrain elemental composition, but different structures can share the same formula."/>
          <outline text="Tandem mass spectrometry" _note="Selecting and fragmenting an ion reveals additional structural evidence that can be compared with standards or spectral libraries."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Electrochemistry and interfaces" _note="1800-present · Electron transfer connects chemical transformations to electrical work, while surfaces and interfaces shape transport, catalysis and dispersed materials.">
        <outline text="Oxidation and reduction" _note="1700s-present&#10;Redox chemistry tracks changes in electron allocation between reacting species. Oxidation states provide consistent bookkeeping but are not generally the actual charges on atoms.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 17.1 Balancing Oxidation-Reduction Reactions. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-1-balancing-oxidation-reduction-reactions">
          <outline text="Oxidation states" _note="Formal electron assignment identifies oxidation-state changes without claiming a literal transfer of whole electrons in every bond."/>
          <outline text="Coupled half-reactions" _note="Oxidation and reduction must balance transferred electrons as well as atoms and overall charge."/>
          <outline text="Oxidants and reductants" _note="An oxidant accepts electrons and is reduced, while a reductant supplies electrons and is oxidized."/>
          <outline text="Disproportionation" _note="One species can be both oxidized and reduced, yielding products with different oxidation states."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Electrochemical cells" _note="1800-present&#10;A cell separates coupled electrode reactions and links ionic transport through an electrolyte with electron flow through an external circuit. Chemical free energy can drive current, or electrical work can drive a reaction.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 17.2 Galvanic Cells. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 17.4 Potential, Free Energy, and Equilibrium. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-4-potential-free-energy-and-equilibrium">
          <outline text="Anode and cathode" _note="Oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode in both galvanic and electrolytic operation."/>
          <outline text="Charge balance" _note="Electrolyte ion movement prevents sustained charge buildup that would stop cell operation."/>
          <outline text="Cell potential" _note="With both half-cell values written as reduction potentials, E_cell = E_cathode − E_anode."/>
          <outline text="Reversible work" _note="For a reversible cell reaction at fixed temperature and pressure, ΔᵣG = −nFE, with n electrons transferred per reaction as written."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Electrode potentials" _note="1800s-present&#10;Electrode potentials are measured relative to a reference electrode and depend on chemical conditions. Equilibrium potentials describe thermodynamic driving forces, while operating electrodes also show kinetic and transport losses.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 17.3 Electrode and Cell Potentials. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-3-electrode-and-cell-potentials&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 17.4 Potential, Free Energy, and Equilibrium. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-4-potential-free-energy-and-equilibrium">
          <outline text="Reference scales" _note="A reported electrode potential needs its reference electrode and conditions to be interpretable."/>
          <outline text="Nernst equation" _note="The reversible cell potential obeys E = E° − (RT/nF) ln Q with dimensionless activities; at overall reaction equilibrium, E = 0."/>
          <outline text="Standard conditions" _note="Standard potentials refer to standard-state activities at a stated temperature, not every solution with a nominal concentration of one molar."/>
          <outline text="Overpotential" _note="Driving a finite electrode reaction rate generally requires a potential displaced from its equilibrium value."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Electrolysis and electrocatalysis" _note="1800s-present&#10;Electrolysis uses electrical energy to drive chemical conversion. Electrocatalysts alter reaction pathways and rates, while product selectivity also depends on electrolyte, potential and mass transport.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 17.7 Electrolysis. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-7-electrolysis&#10;- Bard, A. J., and Faulkner, L. R. (2001). Electrochemical Methods: Fundamentals and Applications, 2nd ed. Wiley. ISBN 978-0-471-04372-0.">
          <outline text="Faraday's law" _note="The theoretical amount of product is charge divided by nF for an n-electron reaction."/>
          <outline text="Faradaic efficiency" _note="The fraction of charge assigned to a particular product measures current selectivity, not overall energy efficiency."/>
          <outline text="Competing reactions" _note="Multiple electrode processes can share the current, so the intended reaction need not consume all delivered charge."/>
          <outline text="Interface and transport" _note="Adsorption, local pH and reactant supply influence measured activity and can obscure intrinsic catalyst behavior."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Batteries and fuel cells" _note="1800-present&#10;Conventional batteries store chemical reactants within a device, while fuel cells receive reactants from outside during operation. Both convert redox free energy into electrical work and face limits from materials, transport and degradation.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 17.5 Batteries and Fuel Cells. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-5-batteries-and-fuel-cells">
          <outline text="Capacity and energy" _note="Capacity measures charge; stored or delivered energy also depends on voltage and operating conditions."/>
          <outline text="Rechargeable chemistry" _note="Charging reverses the intended cell chemistry, but side reactions and structural changes can cause irreversible loss."/>
          <outline text="Fuel-cell operation" _note="Continuous fuel and oxidant supply supports continued operation, with performance constrained by catalysts and transport."/>
          <outline text="Performance tradeoffs" _note="Energy density, power, lifetime, efficiency and material availability must be considered together."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Surfaces and colloids" _note="1800s-present&#10;Interfaces have structures and energies that differ from the bulk. Small particles and droplets expose large interfacial areas, making adsorption and intermolecular forces central to colloidal behavior.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 11.5 Colloids. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-5-colloids&#10;- Israelachvili, J. N. (2011). Intermolecular and Surface Forces, 3rd ed. Academic Press. https://doi.org/10.1016/C2011-0-05119-0">
          <outline text="Surface free energy" _note="Creating an interface generally costs free energy, which helps explain droplet shapes and aggregation tendencies."/>
          <outline text="Adsorption" _note="Accumulation of species at an interface can alter wetting, reaction rates and effective surface charge."/>
          <outline text="Colloidal stability" _note="Electrostatic repulsion, attractive forces and steric barriers can oppose or promote aggregation."/>
          <outline text="Surfactant assembly" _note="Amphiphilic molecules can form micelles or other assemblies when their molecular interactions and solution conditions favor them."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Biochemistry and chemical biology" _note="1800s-present · Biochemistry explains biological structure and function through molecular interactions and reaction networks; chemical biology uses designed molecules to interrogate those processes.">
        <outline text="Proteins and folding" _note="1800s-present&#10;A protein's amino-acid sequence and environment shape its accessible structures and functions. Many proteins form folded domains, while others contain functional disordered regions or switch between conformations.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 3.4 Proteins. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins&#10;- Anfinsen, C. B. (1973). Principles that Govern the Folding of Protein Chains. Science, 181, 223-230. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.181.4096.223">
          <outline text="Peptide backbone" _note="Amino acids are joined by peptide bonds, while side-chain chemistry adds charge, polarity and other interactions."/>
          <outline text="Structural organization" _note="Secondary structures assemble into three-dimensional folds and, for some proteins, complexes of several chains."/>
          <outline text="Folding landscape" _note="Hydrophobic effects, hydrogen bonding, electrostatics and conformational entropy together shape the accessible conformations."/>
          <outline text="Stability and dynamics" _note="Changes in temperature, pH or sequence can alter folding and aggregation, and a static structure does not describe every functional motion."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Enzyme kinetics" _note="1900s-present&#10;Enzymes accelerate chemical reactions by providing catalytic pathways without changing the equilibrium constant of the catalyzed reaction. Rate measurements reveal mechanisms only within the assumptions of the chosen model.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 6.5 Enzymes. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes&#10;- Briggs, G. E., and Haldane, J. B. S. (1925). A Note on the Kinetics of Enzyme Action. Biochemical Journal, 19, 338-339. https://doi.org/10.1042/bj0190338">
          <outline text="Michaelis-Menten model" _note="For a simple mechanism under initial-rate and steady-state conditions, v = V_max[S]/(K_m + [S])."/>
          <outline text="Saturation" _note="At high substrate concentration the simple model approaches V_max = k_cat[E]_total as active sites become occupied."/>
          <outline text="Meaning of K_m" _note="K_m is the substrate concentration at half V_max in this model and is not generally identical to a binding dissociation constant."/>
          <outline text="Regulation and inhibition" _note="Inhibitors, cooperativity and allosteric effects can change measured rate behavior or require models beyond Michaelis-Menten kinetics."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Carbohydrates and lipids" _note="1800s-present&#10;Carbohydrates and lipids supply structural diversity, energy storage and molecular recognition in living systems. Their behavior depends on stereochemistry, functional groups and interactions with water.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 3.2 Carbohydrates. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 3.3 Lipids. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids">
          <outline text="Sugar stereochemistry" _note="Stereoisomerism and ring formation create distinct sugars and anomers with different biological recognition."/>
          <outline text="Glycosidic bonds" _note="The positions and configurations of links between sugars shape the properties of oligosaccharides and polysaccharides."/>
          <outline text="Lipid diversity" _note="Triacylglycerols, phospholipids and sterols have different structures and roles rather than a single repeating polymer architecture."/>
          <outline text="Membranes" _note="Amphiphilic lipids assemble into bilayers whose fluidity and permeability depend on composition and conditions."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Nucleic acids" _note="1800s-present&#10;DNA and RNA are nucleotide polymers whose sequence and structure support information storage, recognition and catalysis. Base pairing is central to copying and folding, but interactions extend beyond ideal double helices.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 3.5 Nucleic Acids. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-5-nucleic-acids&#10;- Watson, J. D., and Crick, F. H. C. (1953). Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids: A Structure for Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid. Nature, 171, 737-738. https://doi.org/10.1038/171737a0">
          <outline text="Nucleotide chemistry" _note="A nucleotide contains a base, sugar and phosphate, and phosphodiester links give a chain its directional backbone."/>
          <outline text="Complementary pairing" _note="Hydrogen bonding and base stacking help stabilize paired structures, with stability dependent on sequence and environment."/>
          <outline text="Replication and transcription" _note="Polymerases use templates to build complementary strands, with fidelity and error correction affecting information transfer."/>
          <outline text="Functional RNA" _note="RNA can fold into binding and catalytic structures, so its roles extend beyond carrying messages from DNA."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Metabolism and bioenergetics" _note="1800s-present&#10;Metabolism couples chemical reactions to maintain living systems away from equilibrium. Free-energy changes depend on cellular composition, so standard-state values alone do not determine pathway operation.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 6.4 ATP: Adenosine Triphosphate. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation">
          <outline text="Reaction coupling" _note="A favorable net reaction can drive an unfavorable step when the steps are linked by a shared chemical mechanism."/>
          <outline text="ATP hydrolysis" _note="The net hydrolysis reaction can release free energy under cellular conditions; breaking a bond alone requires energy."/>
          <outline text="Redox carriers" _note="Molecules such as NAD⁺ and NADH transfer reducing equivalents between metabolic processes."/>
          <outline text="Chemiosmotic coupling" _note="An electrochemical ion gradient across a membrane can power ATP synthesis through ATP synthase."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Chemical probes" _note="1900s-present&#10;Chemical probes perturb or label biological systems to test molecular hypotheses. A useful probe requires evidence of target engagement and selectivity in the context where it is used.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Arrowsmith, C. H., et al. (2015). The promise and peril of chemical probes. Nature Chemical Biology, 11, 536-541. https://doi.org/10.1038/nchembio.1867&#10;- Sletten, E. M., and Bertozzi, C. R. (2009). Bioorthogonal Chemistry: Fishing for Selectivity in a Sea of Functionality. Angewandte Chemie International Edition, 48, 6974-6998. https://doi.org/10.1002/anie.200900942">
          <outline text="Target engagement" _note="Binding or reaction with the intended target must be established rather than inferred solely from a cellular response."/>
          <outline text="Selectivity" _note="Activity against unintended targets can confound interpretation, especially as concentration or exposure changes."/>
          <outline text="Controls" _note="Inactive analogues, independent probes and complementary biological approaches help distinguish target effects from artifacts."/>
          <outline text="Bioorthogonal labeling" _note="Selected reactions can tag biomolecules in complex biological settings while minimizing reaction with native functional groups."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Polymers and materials" _note="1800s-present · Materials chemistry connects molecular and extended structures to bulk properties, combining controlled synthesis with measurements of order, defects and interfaces.">
        <outline text="Polymerization" _note="1800s-present&#10;Polymerization links monomers into macromolecules through different growth mechanisms. The mechanism controls chain length, architecture and how molecular mass evolves during conversion.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry, 31.1 Chain-Growth Polymers. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/31-1-chain-growth-polymers&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry, 31.4 Step-Growth Polymers. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/31-4-step-growth-polymers">
          <outline text="Chain growth" _note="An active chain end adds monomer repeatedly, with initiation, propagation and possible termination or transfer."/>
          <outline text="Step growth" _note="Reactive functional groups on monomers and oligomers combine, often requiring very high conversion for high molecular mass."/>
          <outline text="Molecular-mass distribution" _note="A polymer sample usually contains different chain lengths, characterized by averages and dispersity."/>
          <outline text="Architecture control" _note="Branching, crosslinking and copolymer sequence create structures with properties beyond those of an isolated repeat unit."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Polymer structure and properties" _note="1900s-present&#10;Polymer properties reflect chain chemistry, size, organization and motion over many length and time scales. The same repeat unit can yield different materials when processing or molecular architecture changes.&#10;Sources:&#10;- McMurry, J. (2023). Organic Chemistry, Chapter 31 Summary. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/31-summary&#10;- Rubinstein, M., and Colby, R. H. (2003). Polymer Physics. Oxford University Press. ISBN 978-0-19-852059-7.">
          <outline text="Glass transition" _note="The glass transition reflects a change in segmental mobility and depends on measurement timescale rather than behaving like a simple equilibrium melting point."/>
          <outline text="Crystallinity" _note="Ordered regions can coexist with amorphous regions, affecting stiffness, permeability and thermal behavior."/>
          <outline text="Tacticity and branching" _note="Stereochemical regularity and side branches influence packing and the mobility of polymer chains."/>
          <outline text="Viscoelastic response" _note="Stress and deformation depend on both elastic storage and time-dependent relaxation of chain motion."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Supramolecular chemistry" _note="1960s-present&#10;Supramolecular chemistry studies assemblies held together by interactions between molecular components. Recognition and self-assembly depend on the environment and on competition between possible structures.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Lehn, J.-M. (1987). Supramolecular Chemistry: Scope and Perspectives. Nobel Lecture. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/lehn-lecture.pdf&#10;- Pedersen, C. J. (1967). Cyclic polyethers and their complexes with metal salts. Journal of the American Chemical Society, 89, 7017-7036. https://doi.org/10.1021/ja01002a035">
          <outline text="Molecular recognition" _note="Complementary shape and interactions can favor association, but binding also includes solvation and entropy costs."/>
          <outline text="Host-guest chemistry" _note="A molecular host selectively binds a guest with an equilibrium strength that depends on conditions."/>
          <outline text="Self-assembly" _note="Components can organize into larger structures through reversible interactions and molecular design."/>
          <outline text="Kinetic trapping" _note="An observed assembly need not be the most stable one if barriers prevent equilibration."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Crystals and diffraction" _note="1910s-present&#10;Diffraction records the interference of waves scattered by an ordered material. Structural models connect diffraction patterns to atomic arrangements while accounting for symmetry, disorder and experimental uncertainty.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., and Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, 10.6 Lattice Structures in Crystalline Solids. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-6-lattice-structures-in-crystalline-solids&#10;- Bragg, W. H., and Bragg, W. L. (1913). The Reflection of X-rays by Crystals. Proceedings of the Royal Society A, 88, 428-438. https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1913.0040">
          <outline text="Unit cells and symmetry" _note="A periodic crystal can be described by a repeating unit cell and the symmetry relating equivalent positions."/>
          <outline text="Bragg relation" _note="Constructive reflection from lattice planes satisfies nλ = 2d sin θ for the stated diffraction geometry."/>
          <outline text="Structure refinement" _note="Measured intensities constrain a structural model rather than providing a direct photograph of atoms."/>
          <outline text="Defects and disorder" _note="Vacancies, substitutions and imperfect ordering affect both diffraction signals and material properties."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Nanochemistry" _note="1980s-present&#10;At nanometre scales, large surface fractions and confinement can change a material's properties. Size, shape and surface chemistry are therefore part of the chemical identity of a nanoparticle sample.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences (2023). Quantum Dots: Seeds of Nanoscience. Scientific Background to the Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2023. https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2023/10/advanced-chemistryprize2023-2.pdf&#10;- Brus, L. E. (1984). Electron-electron and electron-hole interactions in small semiconductor crystallites: The size dependence of the lowest excited electronic state. Journal of Chemical Physics, 80, 4403-4409. https://doi.org/10.1063/1.447218">
          <outline text="Surface-to-volume ratio" _note="A larger fraction of atoms lies near an interface as particle size decreases, amplifying surface effects."/>
          <outline text="Quantum confinement" _note="In sufficiently small semiconductor particles, confinement changes electronic energy levels and optical response."/>
          <outline text="Surface ligands" _note="Bound molecules can stabilize particles, influence growth and change interactions with their surroundings."/>
          <outline text="Size distributions" _note="Measured properties reflect an ensemble of sizes and shapes, so a nominal mean size is not a complete description."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Porous materials" _note="1900s-present&#10;Porous solids provide internal spaces for adsorption, separation and catalysis. Zeolites, activated carbons and designed frameworks differ in pore geometry, chemistry and stability.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Li, H., Eddaoudi, M., O'Keeffe, M., and Yaghi, O. M. (1999). Design and synthesis of an exceptionally stable and highly porous metal-organic framework. Nature, 402, 276-279. https://doi.org/10.1038/46248&#10;- Thommes, M., et al. (2015). Physisorption of gases, with special reference to the evaluation of surface area and pore size distribution (IUPAC Technical Report). Pure and Applied Chemistry, 87, 1051-1069. https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2014-1117">
          <outline text="Accessible porosity" _note="A pore contributes to a measurement or application only if the relevant molecules can reach it under those conditions."/>
          <outline text="Zeolites" _note="Crystalline frameworks with molecular-scale channels can distinguish adsorbates by size and interaction."/>
          <outline text="Metal-organic frameworks" _note="Metal nodes and organic linkers form extended coordination networks, some of which retain permanent porosity."/>
          <outline text="Practical performance" _note="Water tolerance, cycling stability, transport and regeneration cost matter alongside surface area or ideal adsorption capacity."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Environmental and green chemistry" _note="1800s-present · Environmental chemistry follows matter through air, water, soil and living systems. Green chemistry uses that understanding to reduce hazards and resource use when chemicals and processes are designed.">
        <outline text="Atmospheric chemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Atmospheric composition changes through emissions, transport, radiation-driven reactions and removal. The same molecule can have different consequences depending on its location and chemical surroundings.&#10;Sources:&#10;- U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. Basic Ozone Layer Science. https://www.epa.gov/ozone-layer-protection/basic-ozone-layer-science&#10;- Seinfeld, J. H., and Pandis, S. N. (2016). Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics: From Air Pollution to Climate Change, 3rd ed. Wiley. ISBN 978-1-118-94740-1.">
          <outline text="Photochemical reactions" _note="Absorption of sunlight can initiate reactions and generate reactive species in the atmosphere."/>
          <outline text="Radical cycles" _note="Reactive intermediates such as OH participate in chains that control the removal or transformation of many trace gases."/>
          <outline text="Ozone by altitude" _note="Stratospheric ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation, while elevated ground-level ozone is a harmful air pollutant."/>
          <outline text="Aerosol chemistry" _note="Suspended particles exchange matter with gases and provide surfaces for reactions, influencing air quality and radiation."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Water chemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Water chemistry depends on dissolved gases, ions, organic matter and interactions with solids. Chemical speciation often matters as much as the total amount of an element.&#10;Sources:&#10;- U.S. Geological Survey. pH and Water. Water Science School. https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/ph-and-water&#10;- U.S. Geological Survey. Alkalinity and Water. Water Science School. https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/alkalinity-and-water&#10;- Stumm, W., and Morgan, J. J. (1996). Aquatic Chemistry: Chemical Equilibria and Rates in Natural Waters, 3rd ed. Wiley. ISBN 978-0-471-51185-4.">
          <outline text="pH and speciation" _note="Acid-base equilibria control the distribution of protonated forms and can change solubility and reactivity."/>
          <outline text="Alkalinity" _note="Acid-neutralizing capacity is distinct from pH and commonly reflects bicarbonate and carbonate in natural waters."/>
          <outline text="Dissolution and precipitation" _note="Mineral equilibria, complex formation and kinetics determine whether elements remain dissolved or enter solids."/>
          <outline text="Redox conditions" _note="Oxygen availability and other electron acceptors influence microbial pathways and the chemical forms of dissolved elements."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Biogeochemical cycles" _note="1800s-present&#10;Elements move among geological, atmospheric and biological reservoirs through linked physical and chemical processes. Human activity can change both the sizes of reservoirs and the rates connecting them.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., and Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, 46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles. OpenStax. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-3-biogeochemical-cycles">
          <outline text="Reservoirs and fluxes" _note="A large stored quantity and a large transfer rate describe different aspects of an elemental cycle."/>
          <outline text="Carbon transformations" _note="Photosynthesis, respiration, dissolution and mineral formation redistribute carbon among compounds and reservoirs."/>
          <outline text="Nitrogen transformations" _note="Fixation, nitrification and denitrification change nitrogen's chemical form and biological availability."/>
          <outline text="Nutrient imbalance" _note="Added nitrogen or phosphorus can change ecosystem growth and oxygen demand, connecting chemical inputs to ecological effects."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Pollutant fate" _note="1900s-present&#10;A released chemical can move between environmental media, transform or persist. Exposure depends on this history, so detecting a substance alone does not determine its full environmental impact.&#10;Sources:&#10;- U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. Fate, Transport and Persistence Studies. https://www.epa.gov/emergency-response-research/fate-transport-persistence-studies&#10;- Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry. Element 2: Environmental Fate and Transport. Public Health Assessment Guidance Manual. https://www.atsdr.cdc.gov/pha-guidance/conducting_scientific_evaluations/exposure_pathways/environmental_fate_and_transport.html">
          <outline text="Partitioning and transport" _note="Solubility, volatility and sorption influence movement through air, water, soil and sediment."/>
          <outline text="Transformation" _note="Photolysis, hydrolysis and biological processes can form products with properties different from the starting compound."/>
          <outline text="Persistence" _note="A degradation timescale depends on medium and conditions, so a single half-life is not universal."/>
          <outline text="Bioaccumulation" _note="Uptake exceeding elimination can increase concentrations in organisms; biomagnification specifically describes increases across trophic levels."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Green chemistry" _note="1990s-present&#10;Green chemistry designs products and processes to reduce hazardous substances and waste at their source. A greener choice needs evidence across the process rather than a single favorable label.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Anastas, P. T., and Warner, J. C. (1998). Green Chemistry: Theory and Practice. Oxford University Press. ISBN 978-0-19-850698-0.&#10;- American Chemical Society. 12 Principles of Green Chemistry. https://www.acs.org/green-chemistry-sustainability/principles/12-principles-of-green-chemistry.html">
          <outline text="Waste prevention" _note="Avoiding unwanted material is preferable to relying solely on treatment after it forms."/>
          <outline text="Atom economy" _note="The stoichiometric mass fraction of reactants incorporated into the desired product complements yield but does not capture solvents, energy or toxicity."/>
          <outline text="Safer design" _note="Choosing less hazardous reagents, solvents and products can reduce risks throughout their use and disposal."/>
          <outline text="Whole-process assessment" _note="Energy demand, feedstock production, separation, reuse and end-of-life outcomes can change the comparison between routes."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Industrial chemistry" _note="1800s-present&#10;Industrial chemistry turns reactions into dependable manufacturing processes by integrating catalysis, separation, energy and material flows. A successful laboratory reaction may need substantial redesign for reliable production.&#10;Sources:&#10;- International Energy Agency (2021). Ammonia Technology Roadmap: Towards more sustainable nitrogen fertiliser production. https://www.iea.org/reports/ammonia-technology-roadmap&#10;- American Chemical Society. 12 Principles of Green Chemistry. https://www.acs.org/green-chemistry-sustainability/principles/12-principles-of-green-chemistry.html">
          <outline text="Reaction and separation" _note="Unreacted feed and by-products must be separated, recovered or treated as part of the complete process."/>
          <outline text="Haber-Bosch synthesis" _note="Catalytic ammonia production combines nitrogen and hydrogen, balancing equilibrium, reaction rate and process integration."/>
          <outline text="Process intensification" _note="Changing equipment or integrating operations can improve heat transfer, mass transfer and resource efficiency."/>
          <outline text="Feedstock and energy choices" _note="The environmental footprint depends on how raw materials and energy are supplied, not only on the final reaction vessel."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Open frontier" _note="Active research; checked November 2026 · These topics connect established demonstrations to unresolved questions of generality, reliability and scale. Dates record a source check, not expert consensus or a claim that every recent paper has been covered.">
        <outline text="Predictive synthesis" _note="1960s-present · open question, as of 2026-11-10&#10;Computer-assisted synthesis now predicts reactions and proposes routes from large chemical datasets. Reliable planning still requires testing unfamiliar chemistry, practical conditions and whether an entire proposed route can be executed.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Maziarz, K., Liu, G., Pultar, F., et al. (2026). Chemist-aligned retrosynthesis by ensembling diverse inductive bias models. Nature, published 21 September 2026. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-11160-9&#10;- Segler, M. H. S., Preuss, M., and Waller, M. P. (2018). Planning chemical syntheses with deep neural networks and symbolic AI. Nature, 555, 604-610. https://doi.org/10.1038/nature25978">
          <outline text="Retrosynthetic search" _note="Work backward from a target to candidate precursors, then assess the feasibility of each proposed transformation."/>
          <outline text="Recent demonstration" _note="RetroChimera (2026) reports improved predictions under tested distribution shifts and chemist assessments, without establishing universal chemical reliability."/>
          <outline text="Data and evaluation" _note="Published reactions omit many failures, and benchmark overlap or narrow coverage can make performance appear more general than it is."/>
          <outline text="Open question" _note="Can a system predict conditions, selectivity and connected multistep routes with calibrated uncertainty and prospective experimental success?"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Autonomous laboratories" _note="2000s-present · open question, as of 2026-11-10&#10;Autonomous laboratories connect experiment selection, execution, measurement and feedback. Demonstrated systems operate within defined chemical spaces and objectives, while broader exploratory reliability remains a research problem.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Burger, B., et al. (2020). A mobile robotic chemist. Nature, 583, 237-241. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2442-2&#10;- Dai, T., Vijayakrishnan, S., Szczypiński, F. T., et al. (2024). Autonomous mobile robots for exploratory synthetic chemistry. Nature, 635, 890-897. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08173-7">
          <outline text="Closed-loop experimentation" _note="Measured outcomes guide subsequent experiments rather than merely executing a fixed sequence."/>
          <outline text="Demonstrated scope" _note="Mobile-robot studies have optimized photocatalysts and combined synthesis with multiple analytical methods for selected exploratory workflows."/>
          <outline text="Measurement validity" _note="An apparently successful signal can hide an unexpected product, so orthogonal characterization and reproducibility checks matter."/>
          <outline text="Open question" _note="Can systems detect instrument or interpretation failures, transfer across laboratories and explore new chemistry with reliable human oversight?"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Molecular design with AI" _note="2010s-present · open question, as of 2026-11-10&#10;Models can propose molecules or predict aspects of their structures and properties. A useful designed molecule must satisfy several experimental requirements that a favorable predicted score cannot establish by itself.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Abramson, J., Adler, J., Dunger, J., et al. (2024). Accurate structure prediction of biomolecular interactions with AlphaFold 3. Nature, 630, 493-500. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07487-w&#10;- Lv, L., Li, H., Wang, Y., et al. (2026). Navigating chemical-linguistic sharing space with heterogeneous molecular encoding. Nature Communications, 17, 8511. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74666-w">
          <outline text="Generative design" _note="Candidate generation can optimize chosen properties, but the result depends on representation, training data and objective design."/>
          <outline text="Structure prediction" _note="AlphaFold 3 predicts structures of diverse biomolecular complexes, which is distinct from measuring binding affinity, dynamics or cellular activity."/>
          <outline text="Multiple constraints" _note="Synthetic accessibility, stability, selectivity and function must be assessed together rather than optimizing one proxy without validation."/>
          <outline text="Open question" _note="Can models generalize to unfamiliar chemical space and produce reproducibly useful molecules through prospective experimental tests?"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Sustainable chemical energy" _note="1970s-present · open question, as of 2026-11-10&#10;Electrochemical and photochemical routes can convert energy into chemical fuels and feedstocks. The open challenge is to combine selective chemistry with durable operation and a favorable complete energy and material balance.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Zhong, S., Sui, P., Holtappels, P., Navarrete, A., Li, F., and Dittmeyer, R. (2025). Robust and efficient electroreduction of CO2 to CO in a modified zero-gap electrochemical cell. Chemical Engineering Journal, 509, 161119. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2025.161119&#10;- International Energy Agency (2021). Ammonia Technology Roadmap. https://www.iea.org/reports/ammonia-technology-roadmap">
          <outline text="Selectivity and efficiency" _note="High Faradaic efficiency identifies where charge goes, but a process can still consume excessive energy at its operating voltage."/>
          <outline text="Device-level demonstrations" _note="CO₂ electrolyzers can achieve useful product formation under specific conditions, while catalyst and reactor design jointly determine performance."/>
          <outline text="Durability and resources" _note="Long operation, impurity tolerance and accessible catalyst materials matter alongside a short high-performance measurement."/>
          <outline text="Open question" _note="Can a complete process, including feed preparation and product separation, provide a scalable low-emission alternative under realistic electricity supplies?"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Circular polymers" _note="1990s-present · open question, as of 2026-11-10&#10;Circular-polymer research seeks materials and recovery processes that preserve useful molecular resources through repeated use. Selective depolymerization and enzyme engineering have produced demonstrations, but their practical scope depends on the waste stream and full process.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Norton-Baker, B., Fram, B., Thadani, N. N., et al. (2026). Iterative computational and rational design generates diverse PET hydrolases for plastic depolymerization. Nature Communications, 17, 11371, published 3 November 2026. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-78471-3&#10;- Tournier, V., et al. (2020). An engineered PET depolymerase to break down and recycle plastic bottles. Nature, 580, 216-219. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2149-4">
          <outline text="Recovery pathways" _note="Mechanical reuse, chemical depolymerization and redesign address different polymers and can complement one another."/>
          <outline text="Enzyme engineering" _note="PET hydrolase studies, including a 2026 design study, improve tested enzymes without establishing equivalent performance across all plastics or industrial feeds."/>
          <outline text="Process boundaries" _note="Pretreatment, additives, mixed materials, monomer purification and energy input affect whether recovered material closes a useful loop."/>
          <outline text="Open question" _note="Can repeated recovery retain performance and deliver lower total impacts at realistic scale, rather than merely achieving conversion in a selected assay?"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Origins of chemical life" _note="1950s-present · open question, as of 2026-11-10&#10;Prebiotic chemistry tests how nonliving chemical systems could have acquired the components and processes needed for life. Individual plausible reactions do not yet reconstruct a complete historical path to a self-maintaining, evolving system.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Patel, B. H., Percivalle, C., Ritson, D. J., Duffy, C. D., and Sutherland, J. D. (2015). Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism. Nature Chemistry, 7, 301-307. https://doi.org/10.1038/nchem.2202&#10;- Singh, J., Thoma, B., Whitaker, D., Satterly Webley, M., Yao, Y., and Powner, M. W. (2025). Thioester-mediated RNA aminoacylation and peptidyl-RNA synthesis in water. Nature, 644, 933-944. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09388-y">
          <outline text="Building blocks" _note="Experiments explore plausible chemical routes to precursors of nucleic acids, peptides and membranes."/>
          <outline text="Connected chemistry" _note="A candidate pathway must account for compatible environments, available feedstocks and how products move between stages."/>
          <outline text="Information and compartments" _note="Replication, selection and boundary formation must eventually operate together rather than only in isolated demonstrations."/>
          <outline text="Open question" _note="How did chemical networks integrate energy use, heredity and compartment growth under conditions that could occur on the early Earth?"/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
    </outline>
  </body>
</opml>
